{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-15_12-22-18-hashicorp_raft-real-run/draft && python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nst=json.load(open('../state.json'))\npar=[i for i in st['interpretations'] if i.get('id')=='983e6d23976b4e4e9eff6a3987874db1-interpretation-1'][0]\nNEW=\\\"01617c5662f34c3eb21857602f6cac4f\\\"\nIDX=[1,3,5,7,9,11]\ninterp={\n \\\"investigation_id\\\":\\\"3b823834c8cb46da98e77d8e988bf5d9-question-0\\\",\n \\\"source_ids\\\":list(dict.fromkeys(par['source_ids']+[\\\"api_apply_future\\\"])),\n \\\"executions\\\":{**par['executions'], NEW:IDX},\n \\\"conclusion\\\":\\\"explained\\\",\n \\\"defect_claim\\\":\\\"asserted\\\",\n \\\"rationale\\\":(par['rationale']+\\\" \\\"\n   \\\"The consequence that this account previously kept conditional is now witnessed. A separate probe registers the verify request while the leader is isolated from every peer, stalls the leader's main loop inside StoreLogs for 900ms so its lease case cannot run, and reconnects only the non-voter once a voter majority has had time to elect. In all six trials a different server was sampled in Leader state at term 3 before the verdict (first at 319-454ms) while the old leader stayed at term 2, and in three of them (result events at parsed indices 3, 7 and 9) the old leader's VerifyLeader returned success at 931ms - the moment the stall released the loop, by which time the non-voter's acknowledgement had already been counted on a replication goroutine. The other three trials answered 'leadership lost while committing log', so the outcome is a per-trial choice between the two ready cases, but the successful ones are exactly the stale-read verdict the API says it prevents: a caller told 'still leader' while a successor already led at a higher term. \\\"\n   \\\"Two details keep this from contradicting the earlier refuted child question, which asked whether a request *queued during* the stall can succeed: it cannot, because the main loop must be running to register the request, and that is why the successful construction registers first and stalls afterwards. The parent defect claim is unchanged; what changes is its consequence, which is no longer conditional on an unobserved interleaving but demonstrated under a stated condition.\\\"),\n \\\"knowledge\\\":[{\\\"entry_id\\\":\\\"@stalled_leader_verify_success_witness\\\",\\\"revision\\\":1,\n   \\\"assertion\\\":\\\"A leader whose main loop is stalled past its lease can return VerifyLeader success after a non-voter's acknowledgement is counted during the stall, even though a successor led at a higher term for the whole interval.\\\"}],\n \\\"answers\\\":{\\\"d704b04e706144dc964ff451359eda19-gap-0\\\":{\n   \\\"explanation\\\":(\\\"Resolved. The legal condition is a leader whose main loop is stalled past its lease while a non-voter it can reach becomes reachable during the stall: configuration routes are not needed. The new probe registered a VerifyLeader request while the leader was isolated from every peer, blocked its main loop inside StoreLogs for 900ms so the lease case could not fire, and reconnected only the non-voter after 250-500ms. In all six trials a successor was sampled in Leader state at term 3 before the verdict (first at 319-454ms) with the old leader still at term 2; in three trials the verify returned success at 931ms, when the stall released the loop and the already-counted non-voter vote was delivered, and the other three answered leadership loss. The earlier bound still explains normal operation: without a stall the lease case is always ready by the time a successor can exist, so the two cannot overlap; the stall is what removes that protection, because the lease check runs on the main loop.\\\"),\n   \\\"resolved\\\":True}},\n \\\"counterevidence\\\":[\n  \\\"The leadership lease still bounds the ordinary case: without a stalled main loop the lease case is served no later than LeaderLeaseTimeout, which is why the six-trial probe needed a 900ms store stall to reach the condition.\\\",\n  \\\"Only three of the six trials returned success; the other three answered leadership loss, so the verdict is a per-trial choice between the ready verify case and the ready lease case rather than a deterministic outcome.\\\",\n  \\\"The witness depends on a non-voter in the configuration and reachable from the stalled leader; the earlier controls show that without any acknowledgement the same call fails (no-peer control) and that a voter acknowledgement is the legitimate path.\\\",\n  \\\"The stalled leader never adopted the successor's term (leader_term_after stayed 2 in every trial), which is what makes the two leaders coexist and the verdict stale.\\\"],\n \\\"limitations\\\":[\n  \\\"The stall is a wrapper around the log store that blocks StoreLogs on the leader's main thread, and the non-voter is reconnected by transport calls; both are supplied conditions rather than faults observed in a deployment.\\\",\n  \\\"The verdict arrives when the stall releases the main loop (931-932ms in every trial), so the success is returned at the first loop iteration after the acknowledgement had already been counted, not while the loop was blocked.\\\",\n  \\\"The caller's subsequent read of the FSM is not exercised; the stale-read consequence is that the API's documented purpose ('to prevent returning stale data from the FSM') is not met, which the witness establishes from the endpoint and the concurrent successor rather than from a data read.\\\",\n  \\\"In-memory transports in one process with 200ms timeouts and a 900ms stall; multi-host clocks and process suspensions are not covered.\\\"],\n \\\"joint_history\\\":(\\\"Two harnesses contribute to this account. The three-partition probe of experiment d704b04e706144dc964ff451359eda19-experiment (receipts b02d5ff23a504a8ea38116085ba8e23c and 107a6d6c816d448da2b7ff6e3145bac1) establishes that VerifyLeader succeeds with only a non-voter's acknowledgement and fails with none, using the same four-server build, the same 200ms timeouts and the same in-memory transports. Experiment 29a71a7377a44fdbac949d4808f7b847-experiment (receipt 01617c5662f34c3eb21857602f6cac4f) reuses that build and adds the stall, the isolated registration and the late non-voter reconnection. The two are compatible because the topology, configuration and suffrage of every server are identical; the later one supplies the additional condition rather than changing a shared premise.\\\")}\nknowledge={\\\"ref\\\":\\\"@stalled_leader_verify_success_witness\\\",\\\"kind\\\":\\\"fact\\\",\n \\\"statement\\\":(\\\"A leader whose main loop is stalled past its lease can return VerifyLeader success while a successor already leads the cluster at a higher term, because the acknowledgement it counts arrives on a replication goroutine and the lease check that would have removed it runs on the stalled main loop. In the retained run the old leader stayed at term 2 while another server led at term 3 from 319-454ms; all six trials sampled a successor before the verdict, and in three of them (parsed indices 3, 7 and 9) the leader's VerifyLeader returned success at 931ms, the moment the 900ms store stall released the loop and the already-counted non-voter acknowledgement was delivered; the other three answered 'leadership lost while committing log'.\\\"),\n \\\"conditions\\\":\\\"Three voters plus one non-voter; the leader isolated from both other voters and from the non-voter while the request is registered; the leader's main loop then blocked inside StoreLogs for 900ms by a client Apply; the non-voter reconnected 250-500ms later; HeartbeatTimeout = ElectionTimeout = LeaderLeaseTimeout = 200ms, in-memory transports in one process.\\\",\n \\\"source_ids\\\":[\\\"verify_leader\\\",\\\"quorum_size\\\",\\\"start_stop_repl\\\",\\\"notify_all\\\",\\\"update_last_appended\\\",\\\"verify_vote\\\",\\\"lease_check\\\",\\\"lease_check_granularity\\\"],\n \\\"executions\\\":{NEW:IDX}}\nd={\\\"rationale\\\":(\\\"Interpreted the new witness receipt and closed the verify question's open gap. The probe registers the verification before the stall, which is the step the earlier refuted child question could not take, and its six trials all show a successor at term 3 leading from 319-454ms while the old leader stayed at term 2; three of them returned VerifyLeader success at 931ms, when the stall released the loop and the non-voter's acknowledgement had already been counted. The gap answer is now resolved with the stated condition (a main-loop stall past the lease plus a reachable non-voter), the counterevidence is rewritten so the lease is described as protecting only the running-loop case, and a new Fact records the witness for reuse. The parent defect claim is unchanged; only its consequence moves from conditional to observed, and the joint history explains how the two harnesses compose.\\\"),\n \\\"knowledge\\\":[knowledge],\n \\\"interpretations\\\":[interp]}\njson.dump(d,open('update.json','w'),indent=2,ensure_ascii=False); open('update.json','a').write('\\\\n')\nPY\n/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/.venv/bin/python -I -B -c \\\"import sys; sys.path.insert(0, '/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/src'); from consensus_assurance.cli import main; raise SystemExit(main())\\\" validate --run /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-15_12-22-18-hashicorp_raft-real-run --submission update.json 2>&1 | tail -2\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
